# head: (cats, weight 1-3, hook, story, then, now)
# cats: P political, W war, E economic, I ideas, R religion, S science/tech, C culture
R = {
"Cabot reaches Newfoundland": ("PE",1,"England's first foothold claim on North America came from a Venetian sailor and a sea full of cod.","John Cabot sailed west for Henry VII hoping to reach Asia, and landed on the rocky coast of Newfoundland instead. He found no spices, but the waters were so thick with cod his crew could scoop them up in baskets.","England did little with the claim for nearly a century, but it never let it go.","Canada's Atlantic fishing culture, and England's legal claim to North America, both trace back to this voyage."),
"Cartier explores the St. Lawrence": ("PE",1,"France found the river that leads into the heart of a continent.","Jacques Cartier sailed into the Gulf of St. Lawrence looking for a passage to Asia and for gold. He mapped the great river, met the Iroquoians of Stadacona, and borrowed their word 'kanata' (village) for the land.","The river became the highway of New France and its fur trade.","The name 'Canada' and the French language of Quebec both start here."),
"Roanoke colony founded, then vanishes": ("P",1,"England's first try at an American colony disappeared, leaving one carved word behind.","About 115 settlers landed on Roanoke Island in 1587. When supply ships finally returned in 1590, delayed by war with Spain, the colony was gone. The only clue was 'CROATOAN' carved into a post.","England learned that colonies needed steady funding, supplies and local allies to survive.","The 'Lost Colony' is still unsolved and remains one of America's oldest mysteries."),
"Jamestown founded": ("PE",3,"A failing company town in a Virginia swamp became the seed of English-speaking America.","The Virginia Company sent settlers to find gold. Instead they found disease, hunger and the powerful Powhatan Confederacy; most early settlers died. Tobacco, planted by John Rolfe in 1612, finally made the colony pay.","Tobacco's hunger for labor soon pulled in indentured servants and then enslaved Africans.","The US traces its political and economic roots, and its original sin of slavery, to Virginia."),
"French found Quebec": ("PE",2,"France planted a city on a cliff that would anchor a French empire in America.","Samuel de Champlain built a trading post at a narrow point on the St. Lawrence. He allied with the Huron and Algonquin, trading guns and goods for beaver pelts that Europe craved for hats.","New France stretched from Quebec down the Mississippi until Britain conquered it in 1763.","Quebec is still the heart of French-speaking North America, and separatism remains a live issue in Canada."),
"First enslaved Africans arrive in Virginia": ("PE",3,"Twenty Africans sold off a ship in 1619 marked the start of slavery in English America.","English privateers seized Angolans from a Portuguese slave ship and traded about 20 of them to Virginia colonists for food. Their status was not yet fixed by law, but within decades Virginia wrote race-based, lifelong slavery into its codes.","Slavery grew into the backbone of the Southern economy and shaped the Constitution itself.","The '1619' date is now central to American debates about how to tell the nation's story."),
"First elected assembly (House of Burgesses)": ("PI",2,"In the same year slavery arrived, Virginia also got America's first elected assembly.","To attract settlers, the Virginia Company let colonists elect representatives to help make local laws. It was the first legislature of its kind in English America, though only property-owning white men had a say.","Colonists grew used to governing themselves, which later made British taxes feel like tyranny.","Freedom and slavery arriving together in 1619 captures the central contradiction of American history."),
"Mayflower lands at Plymouth": ("PRI",2,"A ship of religious dissenters signed a pact to govern themselves before stepping ashore.","The Pilgrims fled England to worship their own way. Blown off course, they signed the Mayflower Compact, agreeing to make and obey their own laws. Half died the first winter; Wampanoag help, through Massasoit and Squanto, kept the rest alive.","Plymouth inspired a much larger Puritan migration to New England.","The Compact is an early model of government by consent, and Thanksgiving is built on this story."),
"Massachusetts Bay Colony": ("PR",2,"A thousand Puritans set out to build a model 'city upon a hill.'","Led by John Winthrop, Puritans arrived in a well-funded, organized migration and founded Boston. They prized education (Harvard came in 1636) and strict religious community, and they banished dissenters like Anne Hutchinson and Roger Williams.","New England grew into a literate, town-meeting society quite different from the tobacco South.","The idea of America as a 'city upon a hill' is still quoted by presidents."),
"English seize New Amsterdam → New York": ("PE",2,"The Dutch gave up Manhattan without a fight, and New Amsterdam became New York.","An English fleet sailed into the harbor during a trade war with the Dutch. With few defenders and little support, governor Peter Stuyvesant surrendered. The city was renamed for the king's brother, the Duke of York.","England now held an unbroken coast from Maine to the Carolinas.","New York's trade-first, many-languages culture comes from its Dutch beginning."),
"King Philip's War": ("W",2,"The deadliest war per person in American history broke Native power in New England.","Metacom, the Wampanoag leader the English called King Philip, led a coalition against colonists who kept taking land and imposing their law. Dozens of towns burned. Metacom was killed and many captives were sold into slavery in the Caribbean.","Native nations in southern New England never recovered their independence.","It set a pattern of frontier war and removal that repeated across the continent."),
"Bacon's Rebellion": ("PW",2,"Poor white and Black Virginians rose up together, and the colony's elite never forgot it.","Nathaniel Bacon led frontier settlers, servants and enslaved people against the governor, demanding war on Native peoples and more land. They burned Jamestown before the revolt collapsed when Bacon died.","Frightened planters turned to African slavery and passed laws dividing workers by race.","Historians point to this moment as a key origin of America's racial hierarchy."),
"Penn founds Pennsylvania": ("RIP",2,"A Quaker turned a royal debt into a colony built on religious freedom.","King Charles II owed William Penn's father money and paid with land. Penn designed Pennsylvania as a 'holy experiment' with religious tolerance and fair dealings with the Lenape. Philadelphia was laid out on a grid.","Pennsylvania drew Germans, Scots-Irish and others, becoming the most diverse colony.","Religious freedom and pluralism became core American ideals, and Philadelphia hosted both founding documents."),
"Salem witch trials": ("RC",1,"Village feuds and fear led a Puritan town to hang 19 people as witches.","After girls in Salem Village had strange fits, accusations spread fast. Courts accepted 'spectral evidence' of dreams and visions, and 20 people were executed before officials stopped the trials.","Massachusetts later apologized and paid compensation to families.","'Witch hunt' became shorthand for mass hysteria, from the McCarthy era to today."),
"Stono Rebellion": ("WP",1,"The largest slave revolt in Britain's mainland colonies marched under drums toward freedom in Florida.","About 60 enslaved people, many from Kongo with military experience, seized weapons near Charleston and headed for Spanish Florida, which promised freedom. Militia caught and killed most of them.","South Carolina passed a harsher slave code and banned enslaved people from gathering or learning to read.","Stono shows that enslaved Africans resisted from the start."),
"French & Indian War begins": ("W",2,"A skirmish led by a 22-year-old George Washington lit the fuse of a world war.","Britain and France both claimed the Ohio River Valley. Washington's Virginia militia attacked a French party in 1754, starting a war that spread to Europe, India and the oceans as the Seven Years' War.","Britain won, but its huge war debt led it to tax the colonies.","Without this war, there's no American Revolution a decade later."),
"Treaty of Paris: Britain wins Canada": ("P",2,"France gave up almost all of North America in a single treaty.","Defeated in the Seven Years' War, France handed Canada and land east of the Mississippi to Britain, and Louisiana to Spain. Britain now dominated the continent's east.","Free of the French threat, colonists needed British protection much less.","Britain's victory set up both Canada's British identity and the American Revolution."),
"Stamp Act": ("PE",2,"A tax on paper sparked the cry of 'no taxation without representation.'","To pay war debts, Parliament taxed newspapers, legal documents and playing cards in the colonies. Colonists boycotted British goods, rioted, and met in a Stamp Act Congress. Parliament repealed it a year later.","Colonists learned that united protest worked, and Britain kept trying new taxes.","The idea that taxes need the consent of the taxed is now a basic democratic principle."),
"Boston Massacre": ("PW",1,"Five dead in a snowy Boston street became the Revolution's most powerful propaganda.","British soldiers fired into a crowd throwing snowballs and rocks, killing five, including Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent. Paul Revere's dramatic engraving spread the story everywhere.","John Adams defended the soldiers in court; most were acquitted.","It shows how one image can shape a political cause."),
"Boston Tea Party": ("PE",2,"Colonists dumped 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor, and Britain cracked down hard.","The Tea Act gave the East India Company a monopoly on tea in the colonies. The Sons of Liberty, disguised as Mohawks, boarded three ships and destroyed the cargo.","Britain closed Boston's port and took over the colony's government, pushing the colonies to unite.","It remains the go-to symbol of American protest against taxes and distant power."),
"Lexington & Concord": ("W",2,"'The shot heard round the world' turned years of protest into a war.","British troops marched to seize colonial weapons at Concord. Warned by riders like Paul Revere, militia met them at Lexington and Concord, then harried them all the way back to Boston.","Within weeks, colonial forces had Boston under siege and the Continental Army was formed.","April 19 is still Patriots' Day in Massachusetts, the day of the Boston Marathon."),
"Declaration of Independence": ("PI",3,"Thirteen colonies declared that 'all men are created equal' and broke from the world's greatest empire.","Fifteen months into the war, the Continental Congress adopted Thomas Jefferson's draft announcing independence. It argued that governments get their power from the consent of the governed, and that people may overthrow tyrants.","France saw a real cause worth backing and joined the war in 1778.","Its words inspired revolutions worldwide and were later used by abolitionists, suffragists and civil rights leaders to demand that America live up to them."),
"Victory at Yorktown": ("W",2,"A French fleet trapped the British army in Virginia, and the war was effectively over.","Washington and French general Rochambeau marched south while the French navy blocked escape by sea. Cornwallis surrendered over 7,000 troops after a three-week siege.","Britain lost the will to fight and began peace talks.","America's independence was won with decisive French help, a fact often forgotten."),
"Independence recognized": ("P",2,"Britain officially accepted that the United States was a free country.","The Treaty of Paris recognized US independence and set its western border at the Mississippi River. Britain kept Canada.","The new nation was free but weak, deeply in debt, and loosely governed.","It doubled the area of the colonies and opened the way west."),
"Constitution written": ("PI",3,"Fifty-five men in a hot Philadelphia room designed a government that still runs the US today.","The first national government, under the Articles of Confederation, couldn't tax or keep order. Delegates wrote a new Constitution with three branches and checks and balances, and struck compromises over slavery, like counting enslaved people as three-fifths of a person.","After a fierce ratification fight, it took effect in 1789 with Washington as president.","It is the oldest written national constitution still in use and a model copied around the world."),
"Bill of Rights": ("PI",2,"Ten amendments spelled out freedoms the government could not take away.","Opponents of the Constitution feared a powerful central government. James Madison drafted amendments protecting speech, religion, the press, the right to bear arms, and fair trials.","Courts slowly made these rights real over the next two centuries.","Free speech, gun rights and police searches are still argued over in Bill of Rights terms."),
"Cotton gin → slavery expands": ("ES",3,"A simple machine made cotton king and slavery stronger than ever.","Eli Whitney's gin pulled seeds from cotton far faster than by hand. Growing short-staple cotton suddenly paid almost anywhere in the South, and planters pushed west into the Deep South with enslaved workers.","Enslaved people in the US grew from about 700,000 in 1790 to nearly 4 million by 1860.","It shows how technology can deepen injustice, and it tied the US South to Britain's textile mills."),
"Louisiana Purchase": ("PE",3,"For about 3 cents an acre, the US doubled in size overnight.","Napoleon needed money after his army was destroyed in Haiti, and gave up on an American empire. Jefferson's envoys, sent to buy New Orleans, came back with 828,000 square miles.","It opened the West to settlement and set off decades of fights over whether new states would allow slavery.","Fifteen of today's states lie partly or wholly in the territory."),
"Lewis & Clark expedition": ("PS",2,"A two-year trek mapped the American West and staked a claim to the Pacific.","Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the new territory. Guided in part by Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman, the Corps of Discovery reached the Pacific in 1805 and returned with maps and scientific notes.","Their journey strengthened US claims to the Oregon Country.","It's the founding story of the American West."),
"Atlantic slave imports banned": ("PE",1,"Congress banned bringing in new enslaved people, but slavery itself kept growing.","The Constitution allowed a ban after 1808, and Congress passed one on the first possible day. Smuggling continued, and the domestic slave trade boomed instead.","Around a million enslaved people were sold from the Upper South to the Deep South, often tearing families apart.","The 'second middle passage' inside the US is far less known than the Atlantic one."),
"War of 1812": ("W",2,"The British burned Washington, and Americans came out more united than ever.","Angry at British seizures of American sailors and trade limits, the US declared war and tried to invade Canada. Britain burned the White House in 1814, but failed to take Baltimore.","The war ended in a draw, but Native resistance in the Midwest was crushed with Tecumseh's death.","It gave the US 'The Star-Spangled Banner' and gave Canada a story of resisting America."),
"Florida acquired": ("P",1,"Spain gave up Florida after Andrew Jackson had already invaded it.","Jackson's troops chased Seminole raiders into Spanish Florida and seized forts there. Weak and distracted, Spain sold the territory in the Adams-Onís Treaty and fixed a border with the US to the Pacific.","Florida became a slave territory and the Seminole wars followed.","The deal shows America's habit of expanding first and negotiating later."),
"Missouri Compromise": ("P",2,"A line across the map kept the Union together, for a while.","Missouri wanted to join as a slave state, which would upset the balance in the Senate. Congress admitted Missouri with slavery and Maine as free, and banned slavery north of the 36°30' line.","It held for 34 years, until the Kansas-Nebraska Act threw it out.","Jefferson called it 'a fire bell in the night', a warning of the Civil War to come."),
"Monroe Doctrine": ("P",2,"The US told Europe to stay out of the Americas, before it could enforce it.","As Latin American nations won independence, President Monroe declared that the Western Hemisphere was closed to new European colonies. Britain's navy, which also wanted Spain out, gave it teeth.","Later presidents used it to justify US interventions across Latin America.","It's still the basis of US policy that treats the Americas as its sphere of influence."),
"Erie Canal opens": ("ES",2,"A 363-mile ditch made New York City the nation's commercial capital.","New York State dug a canal from Albany to Buffalo, linking the Atlantic to the Great Lakes. Freight costs dropped by about 90%.","Midwestern grain flowed east through New York, and towns boomed along the route.","New York's dominance as America's business hub started with this canal."),
"Indian Removal Act": ("PW",3,"Congress authorized forcing entire Native nations out of the Southeast.","President Andrew Jackson pushed the law so white settlers could take Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw and Seminole land, prized for cotton and gold. Removal was supposed to be by treaty; in practice it was forced.","About 60,000 people were driven west of the Mississippi in the 1830s.","It is now widely described as ethnic cleansing and shapes Native sovereignty debates today."),
"Trail of Tears": ("W",2,"The Cherokee won in court and were marched west anyway.","The Supreme Court ruled for the Cherokee in 1832, but Jackson ignored it. In 1838 the army forced about 16,000 Cherokee from their homes into camps and then on a march to Oklahoma.","Around 4,000 Cherokee died from disease, cold and hunger.","The Cherokee Nation rebuilt in Oklahoma and is today one of the largest tribes in the US."),
"Texas annexed; 'Manifest Destiny'": ("P",2,"A new phrase claimed America was destined to stretch from sea to sea.","Texas had broken from Mexico in 1836 and wanted to join the US as a slave state. Congress annexed it in 1845, the same year a journalist coined 'Manifest Destiny'.","Mexico saw annexation as theft and war followed within a year.","The belief that expansion was destiny shaped US policy for a century."),
"Mexican-American War": ("W",3,"The US invaded Mexico and took half its territory.","After a border clash President Polk declared war. US troops captured Mexico City in 1847. Mexico ceded California and most of the Southwest.","Fights over whether slavery would spread to the new land pushed the country toward civil war.","California, Texas and the Southwest's Mexican heritage come from this war."),
"California Gold Rush": ("E",2,"A flake of gold at a sawmill set off one of the largest migrations in history.","Gold was found at Sutter's Mill in January 1848. About 300,000 people rushed in from the eastern US, Latin America, Europe and China.","California became a state in 1850, and Native Californians suffered catastrophic violence.","California's diverse population and its boom-and-bust spirit date from the Rush."),
"Seneca Falls Convention": ("PI",2,"Three hundred people met in a chapel and declared that 'all men and women are created equal.'","Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott, angered at being shut out of an anti-slavery meeting, called the first women's rights convention. Its Declaration of Sentiments demanded equal rights, including the vote.","It took 72 more years for women to win the national vote.","It launched the women's rights movement in the US."),
"Compromise of 1850": ("P",2,"Another deal kept the Union together, but its slave-catching law radicalized the North.","California joined as a free state. To satisfy the South, Congress passed the Fugitive Slave Act, forcing Northerners to help catch people who had escaped slavery.","Anger in the North grew; 'Uncle Tom's Cabin' (1852) became a bestseller.","It showed compromise over slavery was running out."),
"Kansas-Nebraska Act": ("P",2,"Letting settlers vote on slavery turned Kansas into a battlefield.","Senator Stephen Douglas wanted a railroad through the territory, so he let settlers decide on slavery, scrapping the Missouri line. Pro- and anti-slavery settlers poured in and fought ('Bleeding Kansas').","Outrage created the Republican Party, which elected Lincoln six years later.","It's the moment the slavery fight turned violent."),
"Dred Scott decision": ("PI",2,"The Supreme Court ruled that Black Americans could never be citizens.","Dred Scott sued for freedom after living in free territory. Chief Justice Taney ruled that Black people had no rights and that Congress couldn't ban slavery in the territories.","The ruling enraged the North and made war more likely.","It's widely seen as the worst decision in Supreme Court history, overturned by the 14th Amendment."),
"Civil War begins": ("W",3,"Southern guns fired on Fort Sumter, and America went to war with itself.","Eleven Southern states left the Union after Lincoln's election to protect slavery. When Confederate forces attacked a federal fort in Charleston Harbor, Lincoln called for troops.","About 620,000–750,000 died, more than in all other US wars combined until Vietnam.","The war ended slavery and settled that the US is one nation, not a club states can leave."),
"Emancipation Proclamation": ("PI",3,"Lincoln turned a war to save the Union into a war to end slavery.","Using his war powers, Lincoln declared enslaved people in rebel states free. It freed few right away, but every Union advance now meant freedom, and Black men could enlist.","About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union, and Britain and France stayed out of the war.","Juneteenth, now a federal holiday, marks the day the news reached the last enslaved Texans."),
"Battle of Gettysburg": ("W",2,"Three days in Pennsylvania ended the South's last invasion of the North.","Lee hoped a big win in the North would force a peace. After brutal fighting, Pickett's Charge failed on the third day. About 50,000 men were killed, wounded or captured.","The South never invaded again; Vicksburg fell the next day.","Lincoln's two-minute Gettysburg Address redefined the war as 'a new birth of freedom'."),
"Union wins; Lincoln killed; 13th Amendment": ("PW",3,"In one month the war ended, slavery was abolished, and Lincoln was shot.","Lee surrendered at Appomattox on April 9, 1865. Five days later John Wilkes Booth shot Lincoln at Ford's Theatre. By December the 13th Amendment ended slavery everywhere in the US.","Reconstruction began without Lincoln, under a president hostile to Black rights.","Freedom for 4 million people, and an unfinished fight over what it meant."),
"Alaska purchased": ("PE",1,"The US bought Alaska from Russia for $7.2 million, and people called it folly.","Russia needed money after the Crimean War and feared losing Alaska to Britain anyway. Secretary of State Seward bought it for about 2 cents an acre.","Gold strikes in the 1890s and oil in the 1960s made it a bargain.","Alaska gives the US an Arctic coastline, and it sits just 55 miles from Russia."),
"Transcontinental railroad": ("ES",3,"A golden spike tied the US together coast to coast.","Two companies built toward each other, the Central Pacific with thousands of Chinese workers and the Union Pacific with Irish and Civil War veterans. They met in Utah in 1869.","Crossing the country fell from months to about a week, and the bison herds and Plains nations were devastated.","Railroads created standard time zones and the national market."),
"Little Bighorn": ("W",1,"Lakota and Cheyenne warriors wiped out Custer's command.","After gold was found in the sacred Black Hills, the US tried to force the Lakota onto reservations. Sitting Bull and Crazy Horse's warriors defeated Custer's 7th Cavalry in Montana.","The US responded with overwhelming force, and the Black Hills were seized.","The Lakota have refused payment for the Black Hills to this day."),
"Reconstruction ends → Jim Crow": ("P",3,"A backroom deal pulled federal troops out of the South, and Black rights collapsed.","To settle the disputed 1876 election, Democrats let Republican Hayes take office if troops left the South. White Southern governments then stripped Black citizens of votes and imposed segregation.","Jim Crow laws and lynching terror ruled the South for 80 years.","The civil rights movement of the 1950s–60s was a fight to undo this."),
"Chinese Exclusion Act": ("PE",2,"The US banned immigrants by nationality for the first time.","Chinese workers had built railroads and mined gold, then faced racist violence and blame for low wages. Congress banned Chinese laborers from immigrating.","The ban lasted until 1943 and set the template for later immigration restrictions.","It's the root of US laws deciding who may enter based on origin."),
"Haymarket affair; AFL founded": ("PE",1,"A bomb at a labor rally in Chicago gave the world May Day.","Workers striking for an eight-hour day rallied in Chicago. A bomb killed police, and four anarchists were hanged on thin evidence. The American Federation of Labor formed the same year.","The US labor movement turned toward bread-and-butter unions.","May 1 became International Workers' Day almost everywhere except the US."),
"Wounded Knee; frontier 'closed'": ("W",2,"The massacre of Lakota families marked the end of armed Native resistance.","The Army, alarmed by the Ghost Dance movement, tried to disarm a Lakota camp. Shooting broke out and soldiers killed about 250 people, many women and children. The same year, the Census declared the frontier closed.","Native peoples were confined to reservations and pressured to assimilate.","In 1973 activists occupied Wounded Knee to protest broken treaties."),
"Plessy v. Ferguson": ("PI",2,"The Supreme Court blessed 'separate but equal' and legalized segregation.","Homer Plessy deliberately sat in a whites-only train car to test Louisiana's law. The Court ruled 7–1 that segregation was legal if facilities were 'equal', which they almost never were.","Segregation spread across schools, trains, restaurants and hospitals.","Brown v. Board overturned it in 1954."),
"Spanish-American War; Hawaii annexed": ("WP",3,"In one year the US became an overseas empire.","After the USS Maine exploded in Havana, the US went to war with Spain, winning in four months. It took the Philippines, Puerto Rico and Guam and annexed Hawaii.","A brutal war against Filipino independence fighters followed.","Puerto Rico and Guam are still US territories, and America's Pacific power dates from 1898."),
"T. Roosevelt; Progressive Era": ("P",2,"A young, energetic president took on big business.","Theodore Roosevelt became president after McKinley was assassinated. He broke up monopolies, regulated food and medicine, and protected vast lands as national parks and forests.","The Progressive Era brought income tax, direct election of senators and women's suffrage.","Antitrust law and national parks are his lasting legacy."),
"Wright brothers fly": ("S",3,"Twelve seconds over a beach in North Carolina began the age of flight.","Orville and Wilbur Wright, bicycle makers from Ohio, built a powered plane with controls that let a pilot steer. On December 17, 1903, it flew 120 feet.","Within 15 years planes were fighting in World War I.","Today about 4 billion airline trips are taken each year."),
"Model T": ("ES",3,"Henry Ford put the world on wheels with a car ordinary workers could buy.","Ford's Model T was simple and tough. His moving assembly line (1913) cut build time from 12 hours to about 90 minutes, and he paid workers $5 a day so they could afford one.","By 1927 Ford had sold 15 million.","Mass production and car culture reshaped cities, suburbs and oil politics."),
"Federal Reserve; income tax": ("PE",2,"The US built the modern government money machine in one year.","After bank panics like 1907, Congress created the Federal Reserve as a central bank. The 16th Amendment allowed a federal income tax.","These tools later paid for two world wars and the New Deal.","The Fed sets interest rates that move economies worldwide."),
"US enters WWI": ("W",3,"America's entry tipped the Great War and made it a world power.","German submarines sank US ships, and the Zimmermann Telegram offered Mexico US land to join Germany. Wilson asked Congress for war to make the world 'safe for democracy'.","Two million US troops reached France, helping force Germany to seek an armistice.","The US stepped onto the world stage, if reluctantly."),
"Wilson's 14 Points; Senate rejects League": ("PI",2,"America designed the League of Nations, then refused to join it.","Wilson's Fourteen Points called for self-determination and a League of Nations to keep peace. The Senate, fearing entanglement, rejected the treaty.","Without the US, the League was too weak to stop the aggressions of the 1930s.","The UN, founded in 1945, was designed to avoid the League's mistakes."),
"Women's suffrage (19th Amendment)": ("PI",2,"After 72 years of struggle, American women won the right to vote.","Suffragists marched, picketed the White House and went on hunger strikes. Women's war work helped win support, and the 19th Amendment passed in 1920.","Many Black women in the South were still blocked from voting until 1965.","Women now vote at higher rates than men in US elections."),
"Stock market crash": ("E",3,"Black Tuesday wiped out fortunes and began the Great Depression.","Stock prices had soared on borrowed money. In October 1929 they collapsed; banks failed and lending dried up.","By 1933 a quarter of American workers were unemployed, and the crisis spread worldwide.","It led to bank deposit insurance and stock market rules still in place today."),
"FDR's New Deal": ("PE",3,"Roosevelt remade the federal government to fight the Depression.","FDR launched programs to put people to work building roads, dams and schools, to regulate banks and to protect workers' right to unionize.","The economy recovered slowly; full recovery came with World War II.","It created the idea that government is responsible for economic security."),
"Social Security": ("PE",2,"The US created pensions and unemployment insurance for the first time.","Elderly poverty was crushing in the Depression. The Social Security Act paid pensions funded by payroll taxes and set up unemployment insurance.","Coverage expanded over decades, and Medicare was added in 1965.","Social Security is today the largest US government program."),
"Pearl Harbor → US enters WWII": ("W",3,"A surprise attack on a Sunday morning brought the US into World War II.","Japan, cut off from US oil, struck the Pacific Fleet in Hawaii to buy time to seize Southeast Asia's resources. About 2,400 Americans died. Germany declared war on the US four days later.","America's industrial might tipped the war on both fronts.","'Remember Pearl Harbor' still shapes how Americans think about surprise attacks."),
"Atomic bombs; UN founded": ("WSP",3,"The nuclear age and the United Nations began in the same summer.","The US dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and Japan surrendered. In San Francisco, 50 nations signed the UN Charter to prevent another world war.","The US-Soviet nuclear arms race followed within four years.","Nine countries now have nuclear weapons, and the UN has 193 members."),
"Truman Doctrine": ("P",2,"The US promised to help any nation resisting communism.","Britain could no longer support Greece and Turkey against communist pressure. Truman asked Congress for aid, saying the US must support free peoples.","'Containment' became the core of US Cold War strategy for 40 years.","It began a long era of US commitments abroad."),
"Marshall Plan": ("EP",3,"American billions rebuilt Western Europe and tied it to the US.","Europe was in ruins and communists were gaining ground. The US gave about $13 billion (well over $150 billion today) to rebuild factories, farms and trade.","Western Europe boomed; the Soviets refused the aid for their bloc.","It's the model people cite whenever they call for a 'Marshall Plan' for anything."),
"Korean War": ("W",2,"The Cold War turned hot in Korea, and it never officially ended.","North Korea, backed by Stalin, invaded the South. A US-led UN force pushed back; then China entered. The war ended in 1953 near where it began.","About 3 million people died; Korea remains split by the world's most fortified border.","The US still keeps troops in South Korea."),
"Brown v. Board of Education": ("PI",3,"The Supreme Court ruled that segregated schools are unconstitutional.","NAACP lawyers led by Thurgood Marshall argued segregation harmed Black children. The Court ruled unanimously that 'separate educational facilities are inherently unequal.'","Southern states resisted for years; troops escorted Black students in Little Rock in 1957.","It's the legal turning point of the civil rights movement."),
"Montgomery bus boycott": ("PI",2,"A seamstress refused to give up her seat, and a city's bus system went broke.","Rosa Parks, an NAACP activist, was arrested for not moving to the back of a bus. Black residents boycotted the buses for 381 days, led by a 26-year-old Martin Luther King Jr.","The Supreme Court struck down bus segregation and King became a national leader.","It proved organized nonviolent protest could win."),
"Sputnik shock": ("SP",2,"A beeping Soviet ball in orbit panicked the US into the space race.","The USSR launched the first artificial satellite. If they could put a satellite in orbit, they could hit America with missiles.","The US created NASA and poured money into science education; DARPA's work later led to the internet.","GPS, satellite TV and weather forecasting all grew from the space race."),
"Cuban Missile Crisis": ("WP",3,"For 13 days in 1962, the world came closer to nuclear war than ever before.","The US discovered Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba. Kennedy blockaded the island; Khrushchev finally removed them in exchange for a US promise not to invade Cuba and to quietly pull US missiles from Turkey.","Both sides set up a hotline and signed a test-ban treaty.","It's the textbook case of crisis diplomacy."),
"JFK assassinated": ("P",2,"The young president was shot in Dallas, and America's mood darkened.","Kennedy was killed riding in an open car on November 22, 1963. Lee Harvey Oswald was arrested and himself shot two days later.","Lyndon Johnson used the shock to pass Kennedy's civil rights bill.","Polls show most Americans still doubt Oswald acted alone."),
"Civil Rights Act": ("PI",3,"Congress banned segregation and job discrimination nationwide.","After years of protest and violence, including Birmingham's police dogs and fire hoses, President Johnson pushed through a sweeping law against discrimination based on race, religion, sex or national origin.","Jim Crow ended in law, though not in practice overnight.","It remains the foundation of US anti-discrimination law."),
"Voting Rights Act; Vietnam escalation": ("PW",3,"In one year, Black Southerners won protected voting rights and America went to war in Vietnam.","After police beat marchers at Selma on 'Bloody Sunday', Congress banned literacy tests and sent federal officials to register voters. The same year, Johnson sent the first US combat troops to Vietnam.","Black voter registration in Mississippi rose from about 7% to nearly 60% within a few years, while Vietnam grew to 500,000 US troops.","Both still shape US politics: voting rules and distrust of foreign wars."),
"MLK & RFK assassinated": ("P",2,"Two leaders of hope were killed within two months.","Martin Luther King Jr. was shot in Memphis while supporting striking sanitation workers. Robert Kennedy was shot in Los Angeles after winning the California primary.","Riots broke out in over 100 cities; Nixon won the presidency on a 'law and order' platform.","1968 is remembered as the year America seemed to come apart."),
"Moon landing": ("S",3,"Humans walked on another world for the first time.","Apollo 11 landed on July 20, 1969. Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the Moon while Michael Collins orbited. About 600 million people watched on TV.","Twelve men walked on the Moon by 1972; no one has been back since.","Apollo spun off advances in computing and materials and remains a symbol of what focused effort can do."),
"Dollar leaves gold standard": ("E",2,"Nixon cut the dollar's link to gold, creating today's money system.","Foreign governments were trading dollars for US gold faster than the US could supply it. Nixon ended the link.","Currencies began to float, and inflation surged through the 1970s.","All major currencies today are backed by trust in governments, not by gold."),
"US exits Vietnam; oil shock": ("WE",2,"America left Vietnam, and Arab oil cuts quadrupled prices.","The Paris Peace Accords withdrew US troops after more than 58,000 American deaths. Months later, Arab oil producers embargoed the US for backing Israel.","Gas lines and 'stagflation' marked the decade; Saigon fell in 1975.","It showed the limits of US power and the leverage of oil."),
"Nixon resigns (Watergate)": ("P",2,"For the first and only time, a US president resigned.","Burglars linked to Nixon's campaign broke into Democratic offices. Secret White House tapes proved Nixon helped cover it up, and he resigned before impeachment.","Congress passed new limits on presidential power and campaign money.","'-gate' became the suffix for every scandal."),
"Iran hostage crisis": ("P",2,"Students held 52 Americans hostage for 444 days.","After Iran's revolution, students stormed the US embassy, angry that the US had taken in the deposed Shah. A rescue mission failed in the desert.","It helped sink Carter's presidency; the hostages were freed minutes after Reagan was sworn in.","US-Iran hostility has lasted ever since."),
"Reagan presidency": ("PE",2,"Reagan cut taxes, built up the military and pressed the Soviet Union.","After the 1970s 'malaise', Reagan promised smaller government and a tougher line with the USSR. He also later negotiated arms cuts with Gorbachev.","Conservatism became the dominant force in US politics.","Both parties still argue about Reagan's legacy on taxes and deficits."),
"INF Treaty with USSR": ("P",1,"For the first time, the superpowers destroyed a whole class of nuclear weapons.","Reagan and Gorbachev agreed to eliminate intermediate-range missiles in Europe, with inspections to verify it.","2,692 missiles were destroyed by 1991.","The US left the treaty in 2019, accusing Russia of cheating."),
"Dot-com boom": ("ES",2,"The internet economy was born in a bubble.","As the web went public, investors poured money into internet startups, many with no profits. The bubble burst in 2000.","Survivors like Amazon and Google became giants.","Today's tech platforms grew out of this era."),
"Gulf War; Cold War ends": ("WP",2,"A quick war in Kuwait and the Soviet collapse left the US as the only superpower.","When Iraq invaded Kuwait, President George H.W. Bush built a 35-nation coalition and drove Iraq out in 100 hours of ground fighting. That December the USSR dissolved.","US troops stayed in the Gulf, angering Osama bin Laden.","The 'unipolar moment' of US dominance began."),
"NAFTA": ("E",2,"North America became one big free-trade zone.","The US, Canada and Mexico eliminated most tariffs between them. Supply chains spread across borders, especially in cars.","Trade tripled; critics blamed it for lost factory jobs.","It was renegotiated in 2020 as the USMCA."),
"9/11 attacks": ("W",3,"Nineteen hijackers killed nearly 3,000 people and changed the world's security.","Al-Qaeda operatives flew planes into the World Trade Center and the Pentagon; a fourth crashed in Pennsylvania. It was the deadliest attack on US soil.","The US invaded Afghanistan, then Iraq, and launched a global 'War on Terror'.","Airport security, surveillance and two decades of war all came from this day."),
"Iraq invasion": ("W",2,"The US toppled Saddam Hussein on claims of weapons that didn't exist.","The US and allies invaded, saying Iraq had weapons of mass destruction and terror links. Baghdad fell in weeks, but no WMD were found.","An insurgency and civil war followed; ISIS later grew out of the chaos.","It deeply damaged trust in US intelligence and leadership."),
"Financial crisis; Obama elected": ("EP",3,"Wall Street nearly collapsed, and America elected its first Black president.","Risky mortgage lending caused a housing bubble. When it burst, Lehman Brothers failed and banks froze worldwide. Weeks later Barack Obama won the presidency.","The Great Recession cost millions of jobs and homes; governments bailed out banks.","Anger at bailouts fueled populism on both left and right."),
"Affordable Care Act": ("P",2,"The biggest expansion of US health coverage since the 1960s.","Obama's law required most people to have insurance, banned denial for pre-existing conditions and expanded Medicaid.","About 20 million more people gained coverage.","It survived repeated repeal attempts and Supreme Court challenges."),
"Bin Laden killed": ("W",1,"US commandos killed Osama bin Laden in a night raid in Pakistan.","After a decade-long manhunt, Navy SEALs found him in a compound near a Pakistani military academy.","Al-Qaeda weakened, though its offshoots survived.","It closed a chapter of the post-9/11 era."),
"Trump elected": ("P",2,"An outsider with no political experience won the presidency.","Donald Trump campaigned against free trade, immigration and the political establishment and won the Electoral College while losing the popular vote.","US policy shifted toward tariffs and 'America First'.","Populist politics reshaped both US parties."),
"COVID-19 pandemic": ("SE",3,"A new virus shut down the world.","COVID-19 spread from Wuhan, China, across the globe in early 2020. Lockdowns closed schools and businesses, and over 1 million Americans died.","Vaccines were developed in under a year using new mRNA technology.","Remote work, government spending and public trust in science all changed."),
"Jan 6 Capitol attack": ("P",2,"A mob stormed the Capitol to stop the count of an election.","Supporters of Donald Trump, who falsely claimed the 2020 election was stolen, broke into the Capitol as Congress certified Biden's win.","Over 1,000 people were charged; Trump was impeached a second time.","It is a defining event in debates over the peaceful transfer of power."),
"Afghanistan withdrawal": ("W",2,"America's longest war ended as the Taliban took Kabul.","US forces left after 20 years. The Afghan government collapsed in days, and a chaotic airlift evacuated over 120,000 people.","The Taliban returned to power and restricted women's rights.","It raised hard questions about America's foreign commitments."),
"Trump elected again": ("P",2,"Donald Trump won a second, non-consecutive term.","He was the first president since Grover Cleveland to win two non-consecutive terms, campaigning on inflation and immigration.","His second term brought sweeping tariffs and executive actions.","It confirmed populism's grip on American politics."),
}
